{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: round six on the remote-link lane, and I want to be plain that two of these three blockers are claims I published rather than defects codex introduced me to.\n\nCODEX ANSWERED BOTH QUESTIONS I ASKED and ruled to remove PATH. That was my own measurement's implication and I did not act on it: I had measured that all five commands run with no environment at all, and that exec.LookPath picks the git binary from the parent before a child environment exists. What stopped me was that I had reasoned one step ahead about this surface twice in the same night and been wrong both times, so I put it to review rather than narrow a security bound on fresh reasoning at five in the morning. I think that was the right call and I also think it cost a round; both are true, and I would make the same call again.\n\nTHE SECOND BLOCKER IS THE OLD HABIT SURVIVING INTO A LAYER CONTRACT. I wrote that the repository's own configuration file is the one remaining admitted scope. It is not: config.worktree when enabled, and include and includeIf targets both absolute and relative, are all inside that scope and all can carry core.fsmonitor. I wrote \"one physical file\" because that is the case I had exercised. This is the fifth time on this lane, and the first time it reached both an artifact and the architecture page.\n\nTHE THIRD IS MINE ALONE AND I VERIFIED IT RATHER THAN ACCEPTING IT. Go's os/exec appends SYSTEMROOT from the parent environment on Windows whenever the child lacks it, comparing case-insensitively — I read addCriticalEnv to confirm. So an inherited value does reach the child on a platform internal/app builds for, and my artifact's \"everything else absent by construction\" is false there. The implementer had noted this behaviour in a code comment. I read that comment and wrote the absolute sentence anyway, which is worse than not having known.\n\nMY LEAN ON THE PLATFORM QUESTION, since the conditions leave it open deliberately. I would narrow and enforce the supported-platform contract rather than add a Windows boundary regression. Claiming a closed environment on a platform this repository does not exercise is exactly how the sentence became false, and a test I cannot run here would be a second untested claim rather than evidence. But that is a judgement about project scope as much as security, so the conditions permit either and I would take a ruling if you have one.\n\nThe rest is unchanged and codex confirms it: the positive construction closes every demonstrated vector on the tested Unix boundary, the citation lookup stays fail-closed, and all gates are green at the exact head.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Close the three blockers in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict 342e9cfc on head 937389e1, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. (1) Empty the inherited-name set: no environment variable is inherited by these Git commands. PATH goes, because all five current commands were measured to complete without it and exec.LookPath already selects the git binary from the parent environment before any child environment exists — so forwarding it prospectively buys nothing and leaves inherited execution influence. Keep the stated HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME and three GIT_CONFIG pins. Remove whatever allowlist machinery becomes dead, including the case-folding comparison and its duplicate-name edge, unless something still needs it — and if something does, say what. Document the parent's binary selection as a separate deployment trust boundary rather than something this constructor closes. (2) Name the real remaining trust scope in both the code comment and docs/reference/architecture.md: repository-local configuration, worktree configuration when config.worktree is enabled, and include and includeIf targets whether absolute or relative — any of which may carry executable Git behaviour such as core.fsmonitor. Not \"the repository's own configuration file\", which is a claim about one case that was exercised. Add --no-includes to the git config --local read if that subcommand is intended to consult exactly one file, so the narrower contract is in the argument rather than in prose. (3) Settle the cross-platform claim honestly. Go's os/exec appends SYSTEMROOT from the parent environment on Windows whenever the child lacks it, so \"every unnamed variable is absent\" is false there. Either exercise the real status boundary on Windows and state every necessary platform admission including SYSTEMROOT, or narrow the supported-platform contract and enforce it, and make every claim in the code, the artifact and the architecture page name only the platform actually exercised. Do not claim a closed inherited environment on a platform this repository does not exercise. Keep the citation exit-code fail-closed rule and every existing vector regression passing and mutation-sensitive, with their hard controls intact. Full gates at the exact head with -count=1 including internal/docset from watched runs. Publish a fresh complete seven-path artifact set at the new head, each resting on the promise to this request, plus any further path the repair changes. Then request independent review from codex citing the head, the artifacts and this request. Do not merge.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}